artifact.self_contained_problem:
Cosmic rays are charged particles (mostly atomic nuclei) arriving at Earth from outside the atmosphere. Their all-particle differential energy spectrum dN/dE, measured over more than ten decades in energy E, follows an approximately broken power law with several known spectral features:

- the "knee" near E ≈ 3–4 PeV, where the spectral index steepens from about −2.7 to about −3.1;
- the "second knee" near E ≈ 100 PeV, with a further steepening;
- the "ankle" near E ≈ 4 EeV, where the spectrum flattens again;
- a high-energy suppression/cutoff above E ≈ 40 EeV.

Cosmic rays at energies up to and somewhat beyond the knee region (the PeV range) are believed to be of Galactic origin, while the highest-energy events are presumed extragalactic. The mass composition (relative abundance of nuclei from protons to iron) as a function of energy can in principle be inferred from ground-based extensive air-shower observations, but different experiments report mutually inconsistent spectra and compositions, with discrepancies attributed to unresolved systematic uncertainties (hadronic interaction models, detector response, atmospheric effects, etc.).

Problem. Identify and characterize:
(a) the astrophysical sources responsible for producing the population of Galactic cosmic rays with energies in and above the PeV range up to the transition to extragalactic origin;
(b) the acceleration mechanism(s) by which these sources can produce particles with rigidities up to and beyond the knee, in a way consistent with the observed spectral features (knee, second knee, ankle, high-energy cutoff) and their dependence on primary mass;
(c) the propagation processes through the Galactic (and, where relevant, extragalactic) magnetic field and interstellar medium that shape the observed energy spectrum and elemental composition at Earth;
and reconcile these mechanisms with the discrepant ground-based measurements of the energy spectrum and mass composition.

